    "case_summary": "69-year-old female with 16-year history of type II diabetes mellitus. In January 2022, patient began experiencing weakness, depression and drowsiness. Patient's diabetes was well-controlled with dagliflozin and metformin, with usual fasting blood glucose maintained at 4-6 mmol/L. On May 12, 2022, patient was admitted to EICU due to weakness, depression for more than 4 months, faint consciousness and drowsiness for 1 day.\n\nInitial vital signs showed:- Rapid heart rate- Shortness of breath- Temperature: 38°C- Blood pressure: 149/84 mmHg- Mental status: Drowsy, depressed consciousness\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood calcium: 4.86 mmol/L (normal: 2.1-2.55 mmol/L)- Phosphorus: 2.38 mmol/L (normal: 0.81-1.45 mmol/L)- Parathyroid hormone: 8.27 pg/ml (normal: 15-65 pg/ml)- Blood gas: PH 7.32, lactate 15 mmol/L (normal: 0.5-2.2 mmol/L)- White blood cells: 37.81 × 10^9/L (elevated)- Hemoglobin: 91 g/L (low)- Lactate dehydrogenase: 4285 mmol/L (normal: 109-245 mmol/L)Imaging Studies:- PET/CT findings: Splenomegaly with increased glucose metabolism, diffuse increased glucose metabolism in liver, enlarged lymph nodes in multiple regions with increased glucose metabolism- Ultrasound: Bilateral hypoechoic nodules in neck and left supraclavicular fossaPathological Tests:- Bone marrow aspiration: 24.6% abnormal cells, CD4/CD38/ckappa positive, CD45c/CD79a weakly positive- Cervical lymph node biopsy: Multiple immunohistochemistry markers tested including CD2, CD5(3+), CD20(-), CD79α(diffuse 2+), and others",
